permalink: /signed-out/
title: "Signed out | Stefanos Tzortzoglou"
bodyScripts:
  - src: "/scripts/signed-out.js"
    type: "module"
bodyClass: "bg-brand-bg text-brand-text antialiased min-h-screen flex flex-col"
---
{# Sign-out used to drop you straight back on the sign-in form, which reads as a failed login
   rather than a completed action - the same screen you would see if the password had been
   wrong. This says what happened, then offers the two things anyone actually wants next. #}
<section class="container mx-auto px-4 py-10 max-w-md min-h-[calc(100vh-12rem)] flex flex-col justify-center">
  <div class="card p-8 shadow-md text-center">
    <div class="mx-auto mb-5 flex h-14 w-14 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-brand-accent/10 text-brand-accent">
      {% icon "fa-solid-sign-out-alt", "text-2xl" %}
    </div>

    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-brand-text">You are signed out</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-brand-muted">
      Your session has been ended on this device and revoked on the server, so it is closed
      everywhere you were signed in.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-3">
      <a href="/login/" class="btn-primary text-center">Sign in again</a>
      <a href="https://tzortzoglou.eu/" id="leave" class="btn-secondary text-center">Back to tzortzoglou.eu</a>
    </div>

    {# WCAG 2.2.1: a time limit has to be cancellable, and the countdown has to be visible rather
       than something that simply happens to you.

       The sentence and the button are separate blocks, not two inline children of one <p>. As
       siblings they reflowed: "10 seconds" is wider than "9 seconds", so at some counts the
       button fitted on the sentence's line and at others it wrapped below - a control moving
       under the pointer once a second, which is the one thing a cancel button must not do.

       The digit sits in a fixed slot for the same reason, so the sentence itself does not
       change width as it counts down.

       Nothing here is aria-live. A sentence containing a number that changes every second would
       be re-announced every second; signed-out.js announces the offer once, through #status. #}
    <div class="mt-6 text-sm text-brand-muted">
      <p id="countdown" hidden>
        Returning to tzortzoglou.eu in
        {# The number and its unit stay on one line: "8" alone at the end of a line reads as a
           stray digit until your eye finds "seconds" on the next one. #}
        <span class="whitespace-nowrap"><span id="secs"
          class="inline-block min-w-[2ch] text-center tabular-nums font-semibold text-brand-text">10</span>
          seconds.</span>
      </p>
      {# A real target, not a word. 44px tall so it can be hit on a phone by someone who is
         trying to stop a timer. #}
      <button type="button" id="stay" hidden
        class="mt-1 inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center justify-center rounded px-3 underline
               hover:text-brand-accent focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2
               focus-visible:ring-brand-accent">Stay here</button>
    </div>
    <p id="status" class="sr-only" role="status"></p>
  </div>
</section>
